const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is vitiligo?',
    a: 'Vitiligo is a common autoimmune skin condition in which the body’s own immune system destroys melanocytes — the pigment-making cells — leaving smooth, well-defined white patches. It is not infectious and not painful, but it can be visually prominent and psychologically hard, especially on darker skin.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does a melanocortin drug fit in?',
    a: 'Afamelanotide, an MC1R agonist that drives pigment, has been studied as an add-on to standard narrowband-UVB phototherapy. The idea is complementary: phototherapy helps bring melanocyte precursors back into the bare skin, and the MC1R agonist then drives those cells to multiply and make pigment. A randomized trial found the combination produced faster, more complete repigmentation than phototherapy alone in some patients, particularly those with darker skin.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is afamelanotide approved for vitiligo?',
    a: 'No. Afamelanotide (Scenesse) is FDA-approved for erythropoietic protoporphyria, not vitiligo. Its use in vitiligo is investigational — supported by trial evidence but not an approved indication. This page describes the science, not an available prescription.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why pair the drug with light instead of using it alone?',
    a: 'Because the two do different jobs. Repigmentation needs melanocytes to be present in the patch and then driven to make pigment. Narrowband UVB helps mobilize and recruit the pigment cells; the MC1R agonist amplifies their pigment output. Used alone, an MC1R agonist has little to act on in skin that has lost its melanocytes.',
  },
];

