    { name: 'Vitiligo', type: 'MedicalCondition', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitiligo'] },
    { name: 'Melanocortin 1 receptor', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin_1_receptor'] },
    { name: 'Afamelanotide', type: 'Drug', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afamelanotide'] },
  ]}
>
  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      'Vitiligo is an autoimmune disease in which melanocytes — the pigment cells themselves — are destroyed, leaving sharply defined white patches.',
      'That is the crucial difference from a tanning problem: the pigment switch is not merely off, the cells that carry it are gone — so an MC1R agonist alone has little to act on.',
      'The melanocortin angle is a combination therapy: narrowband UVB phototherapy helps recruit melanocyte precursors back into the bare skin, and afamelanotide (an MC1R agonist) then drives them to multiply and make pigment.',
      'A randomized trial found the afamelanotide-plus-phototherapy combination produced faster, more complete repigmentation than phototherapy alone in some patients — most notably those with darker skin.',
      'Afamelanotide is not approved for vitiligo (its approval is for erythropoietic protoporphyria); this use is investigational.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    <strong>Vitiligo</strong> is the melanocortin pigment story told in the negative. Most of
    this site is about tuning the pigment signal — turning <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> up
    or down to make more or less melanin. Vitiligo is what happens when there is nothing left to
    tune: the immune system has destroyed the <strong>melanocytes</strong> themselves, the pigment
    cells, leaving smooth white patches where no pigment can be made at all.<Cite n={2} /> That
    single fact shapes everything about how — and whether — the melanocortin system can help.
  </p>

  <h2>A missing bulb, not a flipped switch</h2>
  <p>
    It is worth being precise about the difference, because it explains why vitiligo is hard. In
    a tanning context, MC1R is a dimmer switch on a working light: push the receptor and the
    melanocyte makes more dark eumelanin. In vitiligo the light itself has been removed.
    Vitiligo is an autoimmune condition — the body’s own T cells target and kill melanocytes,
    and the patches that result are not under-stimulated, they are <em>unpopulated</em>.<Cite n={2} />
    Hand such a patch a pigment-driving signal and there is nothing there to receive it. An MC1R
    agonist, on its own, has no cells to act on.
  </p>

  <h2>The two-step the melanocortin angle depends on</h2>
  <p>
    This is why the melanocortin approach to vitiligo is not a drug but a <strong>pairing</strong>.
    Repigmenting a patch takes two distinct things, in order. First the skin has to be given back
    its pigment cells: melanocyte precursors, which survive in a reservoir in the hair follicles,
    must be mobilized and coaxed to migrate out into the bare skin. Standard
    <strong>narrowband-UVB phototherapy</strong> — the mainstay of vitiligo treatment — is what
    does this. Only once the cells are present does the second step make sense: driving them to
    proliferate and actually manufacture pigment, which is exactly what an{" "}
    <a href="/afamelanotide">afamelanotide</a>-style MC1R agonist does.<Cite n={1} /><Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="Why the drug is given with light, not instead of it. Narrowband UVB brings melanocyte precursors back into the depigmented patch (supplies the cells); afamelanotide, an MC1R agonist, then drives those cells to multiply and make pigment (switches them on). Neither step repigments on its own."
    source="Combination rationale per Lim et al., JAMA Dermatol 2015 (PMID 25230094); MC1R/eumelanin mechanism per the SCENESSE FDA label (NDA 210797). Schematic."
  >
    <RepigmentDuo />
  </Figure>

  <h2>What the trial actually showed</h2>
  <p>
    The idea has been tested. A randomized, multicenter trial compared afamelanotide plus
    narrowband UVB against narrowband UVB alone, and the combination produced faster and more
    complete repigmentation in a subset of patients — the benefit was clearest in people with
    darker skin types, whose melanocytes respond strongly to the MC1R signal.<Cite n={1} /> It was
    not a uniform cure: response varied, the drug added the melanocortin class’s own effects
    (notably generalized skin darkening, which in vitiligo can increase the contrast with the
    patches during treatment), and the evidence base remains far smaller than for afamelanotide’s
    approved use. But it was a genuine demonstration that the pigment switch, correctly timed, can
    help refill what autoimmunity has emptied.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Vitiligo is a useful corrective to the rest of this site’s pigment pages: the melanocortin
    system can command pigment, but only where there are living melanocytes to obey. That is why
    the approach here is combination therapy and why it is still investigational rather than
    approved — afamelanotide’s license is for <a href="/epp">erythropoietic protoporphyria</a>,
    not vitiligo.<Cite n={3} /> For the receptor at the centre of it, see{" "}
    <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a>; for the approved drug being repurposed, see{" "}
    <a href="/afamelanotide">afamelanotide</a>; and for why skin darkening rides along with any
    MC1R agonist, see <a href="/effects">the trade-off</a>.
  </p>

